course: chemistry
lesson: 26
title: Colours of fire: fireworks
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: On Bonfire Night or New Year's Eve the sky fills with red, green, gold and blue. Each colour comes from a different metal packed into the firework, the way each paint in a box has its own colour.
explain: When a metal's atoms get very hot they take in extra energy, and then give it back as light. Every kind of metal gives back its own colour: strontium red, barium green, sodium yellow, copper blue.
explain: You can see the same thing at home when a grown-up sprinkles a pinch of salt into a gas flame: it flashes bright yellow, because salt has sodium in it.
explain: Not all light needs fire. A glow stick, a firefly and the northern lights all glow without burning, because energy is given out as light in other ways.
fact: The colours of fireworks come from different metals: strontium gives red, barium green, sodium yellow and copper blue.
fact: Heat gives metal atoms extra energy, and each kind of metal gives it back as its own colour of light.
fact: Salt contains sodium, so a pinch of salt in a gas flame turns it bright yellow; old orange street lamps used sodium for the same reason.
fact: The sparks of a sparkler are tiny bits of burning iron or aluminium; a sparkler gets hotter than 1000 °C, much hotter than an oven.
fact: A firework's bang comes from powder burning so fast inside a closed case that the hot gas bursts it.
fact: Gunpowder was discovered in China more than a thousand years ago, and it was used for celebrations long before it was used in guns.
fact: Fireworks leave smoke made of tiny particles, and big displays make the air dirtier for a few hours; the bangs frighten many pets and wild animals.
fact: Some party candles have metal salts in the wick, so their flames burn green, red or lilac.
fact: The northern lights are made when particles from the Sun hit gases high in our air: oxygen glows green and red, nitrogen blue and purple.
fact: Bending a glow stick breaks a thin glass tube inside, two chemicals mix, and their reaction gives out light with almost no heat.
fact: A glow stick in the freezer glows more dimly but lasts longer, because cold slows the reaction down.
fact: Fireflies make light with a chemical reaction in their bodies, like a glow stick's; some deep-sea fish make light the same way.
fact: Fireworks and sparklers cause serious burns every year; fireworks are only for trained adults, and used sparklers go into a bucket of water.
exercise: Where does the colour of a firework come from? | Different metals packed inside it | Coloured paper wrapped tightly around it | Paint sprayed on the shell | Coloured smoke from the ground
exercise: Which metal gives fireworks their red colour? | Strontium | Copper | Sodium | Aluminium
exercise: Which colour does copper give to a firework? | Blue | Bright red | Yellow | White
exercise: Why does a pinch of salt in a gas flame turn the flame yellow? | Salt has sodium, which glows yellow when hot | Salt soaks up all the other colours of the flame | Salt melts into a yellow liquid | Salt makes the gas burn more slowly
exercise: Why does a hot metal give out light of one colour? | Its atoms give back extra energy as their own colour | It reflects the colour of the dark night sky above it | It has a coloured coat painted on it | It mixes with smoke of that colour
exercise: What are the sparks from a sparkler? | Tiny bits of burning metal | Little pieces of very hot glass | Drops of water glowing in the heat | Flakes of burning paper and card
exercise: Why must a sparkler be held at arm's length? | It burns hotter than 1000 °C | It drips poison onto your shoes | It gets cold enough to freeze skin | It can float off into the sky
exercise: What makes the bang of a firework? | Hot gas made very fast bursts the case | The colours hitting each other in the sky | Thunder from the clouds above it | The firework hitting a bird
exercise: Where was gunpowder first discovered? | China | Ancient Greece | England | Ancient Egypt
exercise: What is the smoke left after a fireworks display? | Tiny particles and gases from the burnt chemicals | Steam from clouds that the fireworks heated | Dust blown up from the ground by the bangs | Mist made by the cold night air
exercise: Why do some party candles burn with a green or lilac flame? | Metal salts in the wick colour the flame | The wax is dyed a bright colour all the way through | Tiny lights are hidden in the wick | The candle burns a coloured gas
exercise: What makes the northern lights glow? | Particles from the Sun hitting gases in the air | Moonlight bouncing off the polar ice | Fires burning far away in the north | City lights reflected by clouds
exercise: What happens inside a glow stick when you bend it? | Two chemicals mix and their reaction gives light | A tiny hidden battery switches on a little bulb | Sunlight stored inside is let out | Friction from bending makes it glow
exercise: How do fireflies make their light? | With a chemical reaction in their bodies | By swallowing glowing insects they catch at night | By reflecting the moonlight | With tiny fires under their wings
exercise: Why do many dogs hide during a fireworks display? | The loud bangs frighten them | The bright colours hurt their eyes | The smoke smells like their food | They want to watch from cover
exercise: Who should set off fireworks? | Trained grown-ups following the rules | Any child who is careful and has watched before | Anyone with a lighter in a garden | Children if they stand far away
test: Sam's big brother says red fireworks are red because there's red paint inside. What's true? | The red comes from a metal, strontium | He's right, the paint glows when it burns | The red comes from hot red smoke | The red is the colour of the fire itself
test: "sparkler's just a pretty light, it's not even hot, innit". Right? | No, it burns hotter than an oven | Yes, sparklers give cold light | Yes, only the handle is hot | No, but only the smoke is hot
test: If you put a glow stick in the freezer, what happens? | It glows dimmer but lasts longer | It glows brighter and lasts longer | It stops forever and is ruined | It turns into a hard ice lolly
test: A game says fireflies glow because they swallowed tiny stars. What really happens? | A chemical reaction in their body makes light | They store sunlight in their wings all day | Glowing bacteria live on their backs | They rub their legs together to make sparks
test: Gran: "the old street lamps were orange as anything, why tho?" | They used sodium, which glows yellow-orange | They had orange glass fitted in front of the bulb | They burned orange candles inside | Orange light was cheaper to paint on
test: Which pair is right? | Barium gives green, copper gives blue | Barium gives blue, copper gives red | Sodium gives green, strontium gives yellow | Copper gives yellow, sodium gives red
test: "fireworks were invented for war first, then for fun". What's true? | They were used for celebrations long before guns | They were only ever used for war and nothing else | They were invented in 1900 for parties | They were first made for war in Europe
test: Oli: "the green in the northern lights is fire in the sky". What's really glowing? | Oxygen high up, hit by particles from the Sun | Forests burning far away in the north | Green moonlight bouncing off the ice | Gas from volcanoes on fire in the sky
test: After the display Tom wants to bin his used sparklers in the paper bag. What should he do? | Drop them into a bucket of water first | Throw them in the paper bag straight away | Stick them in the grass and walk off | Blow on them and put them in a pocket
test: If a firework had only sodium for colour, what would you see? | A bright yellow burst | A deep, dark blue burst | A mixed green and red burst | No colour at all, just smoke
test: Why is the air dirtier for a few hours after a big display? | Fireworks leave smoke of tiny particles | Fireworks suck all the clean air up into the sky | The bangs shake dust off buildings | The lights heat the air and make fog
exam: Identify the principal origin of the diverse colours observed in pyrotechnic displays. | The presence of different metals within the device | The coloured paper enclosing the device | The reflection of artificial light by clouds | The colour of the smoke produced on ignition
exam: It is given that a small quantity of common salt is introduced into a gas flame. Select the observation most consistent with the established facts. | The flame becomes bright yellow | The flame becomes deep blue | The flame is extinguished at once | The flame becomes colourless
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the luminescence of a bent glow stick? | Two chemicals react and emit light with negligible heat | A concealed electrical cell illuminates a filament | Stored sunlight is released upon bending | Combustion occurs within the sealed tube
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a sparkler must be held at arm's length. | Its temperature exceeds one thousand degrees Celsius | It emits a poisonous liquid during use | It may ascend into the air without warning | It becomes intensely cold upon ignition
